.TH GUNTI 1 "2026-09-04" "gunti 0.18.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
gunti \- minimal terminal file manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gunti
.RI [ directory ]
.br
.B gunti \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gunti
lists a directory in the terminal and moves through it with the keyboard.
It browses, sorts, searches, and does the usual file operations. Anything it
does not do is bound to a shell command in the config file.
.PP
With no argument it opens the current directory. Given a directory, it opens
that instead. A path that does not exist, is not a directory, or cannot be read
is reported on standard error and exits 1.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version, licence and project URL, then exit. Nothing is drawn and the
terminal is not touched, so this is safe in a pipe.
.SH KEYS
.SS Moving
.TP
.B j, k
Down, up.
.TP
.B g, G
Top, bottom.
.TP
.B h
Parent directory.
.TP
.B l
Enter a directory, or open a file in
.BR $EDITOR .
.TP
.B c
Prompt for a path and go there.
.B ~
is accepted.
.TP
.B R
Re-read the directory from disk.
.SS Finding
.TP
.B /
Prompt for text and jump to the next matching entry. Case insensitive, matches
anywhere in the name.
.TP
.B n, N
Next match, previous match. Both wrap.
.TP
.B s
Cycle the sort order: name, size, time. Size sorts largest first, time newest
first. Directories always group at the top.
.TP
.B .
Show or hide dotfiles.
.SS Acting
.TP
.B space
Tick the highlighted entry and step down.
.BR d ,
.BR y ,
.B x
and
.B m
then act on every ticked entry. With nothing ticked they act on the highlighted
one. Ticks clear whenever the listing reloads.
.TP
.B a
Create. End the name with
.B /
to make a directory. Refuses to overwrite an existing name.
.TP
.B r
Rename, prompting with the current name.
.TP
.B d
Delete, after confirmation. Non-empty directories are refused.
.TP
.B m
Change permissions. Octal only, such as
.BR 644 .
.TP
.B y, x, p
Copy, cut, paste. Pasting prompts before overwriting anything.
.TP
.B v
Page through a file. This is a default binding, not built in, and can be
replaced.
.TP
.B !
Prompt for a shell command and run it. See
.B SHELL COMMANDS
below.
.SS Other
.TP
.B ?
Show the key list.
.TP
.B q
Quit. Ctrl-C also quits.
.PP
Arrow keys work as
.BR hjkl ,
backspace as
.BR h ,
and enter as
.BR l .
.SH CONFIGURATION
.B gunti
reads
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/gunti/config
if that variable is set, otherwise
.IR ~/.config/gunti/config .
No file means defaults. A line it cannot parse is reported with its line number
and the rest of the file still applies.
.PP
Blank lines and lines beginning with
.B #
are ignored.
.SS Options
.PP
.nf
set sort    name|size|time
set hidden  true|false
set color   dir|link|exec|broken <colour>
.fi
.PP
Colours are
.BR black ", " red ", " green ", " yellow ", " blue ", " magenta ", " cyan ", "
.BR white ,
the same eight prefixed with
.BR bright- ,
and
.BR none .
Defaults match
.BR ls (1):
blue directories, cyan symlinks, green executables, red broken symlinks. The
highlighted row is not coloured, because reverse video over a colour turns the
colour into a background.
.PP
There is no
.B set editor
option.
.B $EDITOR
already does that.
.SS Bindings
.PP
.nf
map <key> <shell command>
.fi
.PP
A binding overrides the built-in key. Built-in actions cannot be bound to other
keys; only shell commands can be bound.
.SH SHELL COMMANDS
Commands run through
.BR sh (1).
Selected files are passed as positional arguments, never substituted into the
command text, so names containing spaces, quotes, newlines or leading dashes
pass through intact.
.TP
.B \(dq$@\(dq
The ticked files, or the highlighted one if none are ticked.
.TP
.B $f
The highlighted file.
.TP
.B $d
The current directory.
.PP
A
.B !
before the command hands over the terminal and waits for a keypress afterwards.
Use it for anything that prints or asks something. Without it the command runs
unseen, which is what a clipboard copy wants.
.PP
.nf
map D  rm \-rf \-\- "$@"
map Y  wl\-copy < "$f"
map L  !less "$f"
map R  !read \-p "new name: " n && mv \-\- "$f" "$n"
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B EDITOR
Used by
.BR l
on a regular file. Falls back to
.BR vi .
May contain arguments.
.TP
.B PAGER
Used by the default
.B v
binding. Falls back to
.BR less .
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
If set to any value, all colour is disabled. This is checked after the config,
so it cannot be overridden by a
.B set color
line.
.TP
.B GUNTI_CD
If set to a file path, the directory
.B gunti
ended in is written there on exit. Unset, nothing is written. No process can
change its parent shell's directory, so a wrapper function is needed:
.PP
.nf
gunti() {
  local d target
  d=$(mktemp)
  GUNTI_CD="$d" command gunti "$@"
  target=$(cat "$d"); rm \-f "$d"
  [ \-n "$target" ] && cd "$target"
}
.fi
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the config is looked for. Defaults to
.IR ~/.config .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/gunti/config
Configuration.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal exit.
.TP
.B 1
The directory given on the command line could not be opened.
.TP
.B 128+n
Killed by signal
.IR n .
The terminal is restored first.
.SH LIMITATIONS
No progress or cancellation on large copies; the display blocks until done.
Deleting a non-empty directory fails, by design; bind
.B rm \-rf
for the other behaviour. Cutting across filesystems fails, because
.BR rename (2)
cannot move between mount points. Built-in keys cannot be remapped. No image
previews, tabs, panes, bookmarks, bulk rename, mouse, icons or plugins, none of
which are planned. Wide character widths cover CJK, Hangul, fullwidth forms and
the common emoji blocks, not the full Unicode tables. Filenames are assumed to
be UTF-8. Linux only.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ls (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR less (1),
.BR rename (2)
